{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>to those who love Me and keep My commandments</b> It appears from the plain sense of the verse that this is a promise concerning these commandments that He mentioned: that He performs <b>kindness to thousands of those who love Him</b> — these are the ones who surrender their lives for Him, for those who acknowledge the honored Name and His divinity alone and deny every foreign god, and will not worship them even at the risk of their lives, are called \"those who love Him,\" for this is the love with which we are obligated with our very souls, as He said: \"and you shall love YHWH your Elohim with all your heart and with all your soul\" (Deuteronomy 6:5) — that you surrender your soul and your life out of love for Him, so that you do not exchange Him for any other el, nor associate with Him any foreign el. And therefore it is said of Abraham: \"the seed of Abraham My lover\" (Isaiah 41:8), because he surrendered his life rather than worship idolatry in the furnace of the Chaldees. And the rest of the righteous are called keepers of His commandments.<br><br>And many have explained (see Maimonides in his Commentary on the Mishnah, Sanhedrin, beginning of chapter 10) that \"those who love Him\" are those who serve out of love, not on condition to receive reward, as the Sages mentioned (Sifrei Devarim 11:13). And I found in the Mekhilta (here): \"to those who love Me\" — this is Abraham and those like him. \"And to those who keep My commandments\" — these are the prophets and the elders. Rabbi Nathan says: \"to those who love Me and to those who keep My commandments\" — these are they who dwell in the land of Israel and surrender their lives for the commandments. \"Why are you going out to be killed? Because I circumcised my son. Why are you going out to be burned? Because I read in the Torah. Why are you going out to be crucified? Because I ate the matzah. Why are you being beaten with a scourge? Because I took the lulav.\" And he says (Zechariah 13:6): \"with which I was wounded in the house of My friends\" — these wounds caused me to love my Father in heaven. We see that Rabbi Nathan explained that love is the surrender of one's life for a commandment. And the verse is surely about idolatry, for by it we are obligated to be killed rather than transgress at all times, forever; but he broadened the matter to all the commandments, because in a time of religious persecution we are killed for all of them, from the other verse: \"and you shall not profane My holy name\" (Leviticus 22:32). And the explanation that was given — \"this is Abraham\" and \"these are the prophets\" — it is not correct to say that the prophets acted on condition to receive reward; but there is in this a sod. He said that Abraham surrendered his life with love, as in the matter that is written: \"kindness to Abraham\" (Micah 7:20), and the rest of the prophets with might (גבורה). And understand this:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "ת\"ק appears in some witnesses; resolved here as the abbreviation for 'the explanation' (הפירוש) based on context. The final kabbalistic hint about love (חסד/אהבה) versus might (גבורה) is left cryptic per instructions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:11:26.857305336Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}